<h1>
Convergence of Digital Twin and Human-Data Interaction: Advancing Customer Experience in the Built Environment
</h1>

<p class="lead">
<h2>Buildings shape our lives, but do we design buildings around people?</h2>
In modern society, individuals spend over 90% of their everyday lives inside buildings. However, buildings are still primarily designed around engineering performance indicators,
such as structural integrity and energy efficiency rather than human experiences.
Architects optimise building space with aesthetics, while engineers monitor electricity consumption, HVAC systems and maintenance schedules. 
However, one important question remains largely unanswered:
<em> how should buildings understand and respond to the people living inside them?</em>

<p>
Poor building layouts influence far more than energy bills. They affect collaboration, productivity, mental wellbeing and even public health.
During the COVID-19 pandemic, inadequate ventilation accelerated virus transmission in indoor environments, 
demonstrating that building design has direct societal consequences (Burridge et al., 2021).
As buildings become increasingly intelligent, the next challenge is no longer collecting more data, it is about the integration of building designs and human desires.
</p>

<h2>Where is the human in today's digital twin?</h2>
Abbott (1955) aruged that <em>“what people really desire are not products but satisfying experiences." </em>
Over years, we have put substantial efforts on optimising building asset as a physical product
rather than a dyanmic space which cultivate satisfying experiences. 
most existing digital twin applications focus on individual building components: 

<ul>
<li>HVAC optimisation</li>
<li>Energy monitoring</li>
<li>Facility maintenance</li>
<li>Structural health monitoring</li>
</ul>

We can model different parts of a building in siloed digital twins and each digital twin performs well in its own specialised tasks.
For instance, twinning the electrical pipes for optimising consumption,
or twinning the heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (HVAC) units to track energy use, temperature regulation and air quality.

Yet, these siloed digital twins are limited in connecting with the occupants inside the buildings, where the human experiences are largely not invovled in today's digital infrastrcuture and building designs.
</p>

<h2>From digital twins to a digital twin system</h2>
<p>We ask "where is the human in today's digital twin"? To answer this question, we conducted an extensive
literature review together with expert focus groups to rethink how digital twins should interact with humans.

Instead of viewing digital twins as isolated engineering tools, we propose a "digital twin system" for advancing human-data interaction in the built environment 
with 3 key dimensions and 26 use cases (see Table 1):

<ul>
<li>Building dimension: building design</li>
<li>Virtual dimension: digital model and data</li>
<li>People dimension: customer engagement</li>
</ul>

