<p>
Connecting these 3 dimensions enables digital twins to move beyond operational efficiency.
Rather than presenting facility managers with dashboards full of disconnected sensor readings, 
a digital twin system becomes a decision-support platform that understand both buildings and people.
Future digital twin systems can support:

<ul>
<li>Personalised indoor experiences</li>
<li>Adaptive workspaces</li>
<li>Social well-being</li>
<li>Human-centric building designs</li>
</ul>

</p>

<h2>2 caveats in human-centric digital twin system</h2>

<ul>
<li>Personal privacy: are users willing to share their personal data to advance building designs?</li> 
<li>Cybersecurity: how can we protect digital infrastructure against cyberattacks?</li>
</ul>

<p>
From our analysis, the 3 proposed dimensions in Table 1 should be stitched together to realise the strengths of both human and artificial intellgience.
The conceptual model in Figure 2 depicts the key elements in each digital twin layer to enhance human-data interaction in the built environment.
This is the beginning of a paradigm shift from a "product-centric" digital twin to a "human-centric" digital twin system.
</p>

